    "origin_id": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/j-b-hunt-sees-cost-pressures-ease-touts-dedicated-wins",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/j-b-hunt-sees-cost-pressures-ease-touts-dedicated-wins",
    "origin_publisher": "FreightWaves",
    "published_at": "2026-11-18T14:27:51+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-18T14:27:51+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-18T14:42:03.222473+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-18T14:42:03.222473+00:00",
    "body": "J.B. Hunt Transport Services said Tuesday it remains focused on “disciplined growth” even though it is seeing outsized demand for its services amid scrutiny of and attrition within the supply side . It believes a measured approach positions it best to restore margins through price increases.\nSONAR: Outbound Tender Rejection Index (OTRI.USA) for 2026 (blue shaded area), 2025 (yellow line), 2024 (green line) and 2023 (pink line). A proxy for truck capacity, the tender rejection index shows the number of loads being rejected by carriers. Current tender rejections show a tight truckload market. To learn more about SONAR, click here .\nCost pressures have eased since September\nThe multimodal transportation provider has seen its price-cost relationship improve since issuing an earnings warning in September, but some growth-related costs will likely linger through the early phase of the upcycle.\n“We are hiring drivers at a pace that’s equivalent to where we were at the peak of Covid,” CFO Brad Delco told investors at the Stephens Annual Investment Conference in Nashville, Tennessee.\nJ.B. Hunt ( NASDAQ: JBHT ) incurred costs from adding several driver-hiring associates at the end of the summer, alongside an undisclosed number of new drivers. While these onboarding efforts generated temporary cost and productivity headwinds, the new recruiting team has reached target performance levels. Ongoing technology investments are enabling the company to fulfill driver demand during the current upcycle with a 30% smaller staff.\nDelco said some cost buckets are up by mid-single- to low-double digits, and that these increases will have to be recouped through pricing in order to restore margins and justify capacity additions. Surging fuel costs have resulted in a 100-basis-point margin headwind this year, but volatility on that cost line has calmed in recent weeks. However, elevated group medical costs continue to be a headwind across all industries.\nThe company has removed $135 million in annual costs (about 1%) over the past year, and reminded investors that cost management is “a continuous event.” J.B. Hunt’s earnings turnaround started in the 2025 third quarter.\nEquipment costs, Montgomery ruling push dedicated demand to new high\nJ.B. Hunt’s dedicated pipeline remains at an all-time high even as it continues to convert prospects into new accounts at an accelerated pace. The company sold service on 425 trucks in the third quarter (970 trucks year-to-date), putting it on pace to exceed its annual target for net truck additions of 800 to 1,000.\nThe company’s dedicated sales team focuses on converting private fleets into J.B. Hunt assets.\nMany businesses are no longer willing to fund non-core capex (tractors and trailers) as equipment costs have increased. Also, the Supreme Court’s broker-liability ruling has shippers rethinking liability risk, with the group becoming more willing to outsource the driver-vetting function to an operator like J.B. Hunt. Further, driver recruiting in an upcycle is difficult for private fleets, which don’t carry specialized hiring teams.\nStartup costs from the recent account wins, however, will likely be a modest drag on margins in the near term. Dedicated contracts are typically structured as five-year deals, with breakeven results taking approximately six months to achieve.\nBNSF temporary outage produces minimal Q4 volume headwind\nManagement also quantified the impact from flooding along BNSF’s (Berkshire Hathaway, NYSE: BRK-B ) Southern Transcon line. It said the heavy rains resulted in a three-day outage, impacting approximately 7,500 loads. That represents roughly 1% of J.B. Hunt’s 550,000 quarterly intermodal shipments. There were no material cost impacts from the event.\nSONAR: Intermodal Contract Savings Index (IMCSI.USA). The IMCSI shows the savings percentage between domestic intermodal contract rate per mile and truckload contract rate per mile. The comparison includes fuel surcharges.\nThe company’s intermodal bid season begins in October each year. It said intermodal is currently 30% to 35% cheaper than truckload. Inflections in TL pricing typically lead changes in intermodal pricing by six to nine months, placing J.B. Hunt in an advantageous position as truck pricing has been on the rise for nearly a year.\nIt said last week at a separate investor conference that the market will likely be supportive of inflation-plus pricing. However, it doesn’t expect intermodal to fully close the pricing gap to truck as TL rates continue to reset higher.\nThe company reprices 10% of its intermodal book in the fourth quarter and 30% in each of the remaining three quarters.\nShares of JBHT were down 3.4% in Tuesday trading compared to the S&P 500, which was off 1%.\nWhy it matters? Driven by heightened regulatory enforcement and the Supreme Court’s broker-liability ruling, shippers are increasingly outsourcing trucking functions to dedicated fleets.\nMore FreightWaves articles by Todd Maiden:\nBroker RXO says TL spot rates not entering deflationary phase\nOctober TL rates surge as shipment counts extend positive streak\nForward Air pauses sale of intermodal unit",
